  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nQuiet and unseasonably mild weather will dominate through Friday \nunder persistent southerly return flow. As lee troughing deepens over\nthe High Plains today, the surface pressure gradient tightens across\neastern and north-central Kansas. Afternoon mixing will tap into \nstronger flow aloft, pushing sustained southerly winds around 20 mph\nwith gusts to 40 mph, strongest across north central Kansas. \nAbundant sunshine and warm advection will boost highs into the mid \n60s area-wide, while afternoon minimum relative humidity dips into \nthe 30 to 45 percent range. \n\nAfter a brief relaxation of the pressure gradient on Thursday \nyields lighter southerly winds (10-15 mph) and highs in the upper \n60s, southerly flow ramps back up Friday ahead of the next \napproaching upper-level trough. Strong southerly wind will transport\ndeeper low-level moisture northward setting the stage for rain\nSaturday.\n\nSaturday as the upstream trough and attendant surface cold front \nprogress across Kansas, increasing large-scale ascent and moisture \nconvergence support high rain probabilities (up to 75 percent) \nspreading from west to east during the day Saturday, lingering into \nSaturday night across eastern Kansas. Temperatures Saturday will \nexhibit a notable northwest to southeast gradient as frontal timing \ndictates daytime warming, ranging from upper 50s near Concordia to \nmid 60s ahead of the boundary in east central Kansas. \n\nFollowing Saturday night's frontal passage, strong cold air \nadvection will usher in a markedly colder airmass for Sunday through\nWednesday. Afternoon highs drop into the upper 40s to near 50 F on \nSunday, cooling further into the low to mid 40s by Tuesday and \nWednesday. Overnight lows will fall into the upper 20s and low 30s \neach night from Sunday night onward, bringing widespread freezing \ntemperatures back to the entire area.",
